# Git Delivery

Origin: adapted from a protocol of the reference donor-harness (push turned into a negotiation;
rotten rebase on SessionStart). Complements the git-doctor hook (detection).

## 1. Commit contract (of this repo)

- The flow is **commit directly to main** (no PR).{% if git_remote_url %} Confirmed: remote
  `{{ git_remote_url }}`.{% endif %}
- **PUSH is an outward-facing action{% if client_name %} (client repo){% endif %}: only with explicit authorization
  from the project owner ({{ owner_name }}).** A local commit is fine; publishing requires the "you may push".
- Commit scope: EXPLICIT staging of the task's files. NEVER a blind `git add -A`
  — repos usually have pre-existing untracked files (deps, tool caches)
  that are not part of the task.
- Mandatory trailer: `Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>` (adjust the
  model name if this project uses a specific alias).
- Skills/secrets: `.claude/` versions ONLY hooks/, settings.json and the non-sensitive
  skills (see .gitignore). Client/business-specific skills stay out.

## 2. Push credential ladder (stop at the first that works)

1. **SSH BatchMode** (current rung — works today):
   `ssh -T -o BatchMode=yes git@github.com` → "Hi {% if git_remote_owner %}{{ git_remote_owner }}{% else %}<user>{% endif %}!" = ok → `git push`.
2. **gh CLI**: `gh auth status` ok → `git push` (gh becomes the credential helper). If
   not installed/logged in: run `.harness/hooks/provision-push.sh` if this project has that script.
3. **git credential fill** (HTTPS): `printf 'protocol=https\nhost=github.com\n\n' | git credential fill` returns a password → push over HTTPS.
4. **flip SSH↔HTTPS**: if the SSH-remote fails but there is a PAT, switch the remote URL
   to https and use the PAT; revert afterward.
   Before saying "I can't push": ALL 4 rungs tried and reported.

## 3. Post-push

`git rev-list --count @{upstream}..HEAD` == 0 confirms it went up. Report the SHA.

## 4. Rotten state (git-doctor covers this on SessionStart, if this project has that hook)

A pending rebase/merge breaks resume. If git-doctor warned: resolve it
(`git rebase --abort` / `git merge --abort`) BEFORE new work; the dirty working
tree was already saved in `git stash` (recover it with `git stash pop`).
